{# ================================================================== METHOD #}
<section class="page">
  {{ head("How we built our numbers") }}
  <h1>How we built our numbers</h1>
  <div class="row method" style="margin-top:12px">
    <div class="col">
      <h3>The data</h3>
      <p>Daily net sales and checks for every café from Toast POS (after discounts; excluding tax, tips and gift-card sales), {{ n.first_day.strftime("%B %Y") }} to {{ n.last_day.strftime("%B %-d, %Y") }}: {{ "{:,}".format(n.store_days) }} café-days.</p>
      <p>The budget's 2026 estimates match these figures within 2% café by café, so both sides are measuring the same sales.</p>
      <h3>Growth measures</h3>
      <p>Growth always compares the same days, months or quarters a year apart. Checks count guests' tickets; average check = net sales ÷ checks.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="col">
      <h3>Our forecast</h3>
      <p>Three standard forecasting models (Prophet, SARIMA and Holt-Winters) are fitted to each café separately and added up. Their settings were tuned on 2025 and tested on January–September 2026.</p>
      <p><b>Our plan</b> is the blend of the three, shaded {{ "%.1f" | format((1 - n.plan_cfg.factor) * 100) }}% down: over-forecasting costs us about {{ n.plan_cfg.over_cost }}× more than under-forecasting, so the plan leans low on purpose.</p>
      <p><b>Track record:</b> forecasting January–September 2026 from September 2025, the blend came in {{ n.track.bias | apct }} {{ "below" if n.track.bias < 0 else "above" }} actual sales across the {{ n.track.cafes | words }} cafés with two years of history at the time.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="col">
      <h3>What the models cannot see</h3>
      <p>New competitors, remodels, openings, closures and price changes. We treat these as judgment calls and show them as separate lines.</p>
      <h3>No growth after September</h3>
      <p>Two estimates of 2027 at today's level: Jul–Sep 2026 sales adjusted for each café's seasonal pattern and annualised; and each café's Prophet trend held at its September 30 level.</p>
      <h3>New-café track record</h3>
      <p>Sales in the first 8, 10 and 12 months from the first trading day of Newport, Beverly Hills, Brentwood and Carlsbad, restated at 2026 average checks.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="note pin-bottom">Budget figures come from the budget workbook (2027_Sales_budget_Investors.xlsx), imported by src/import_budget.py into data/cfo_budget_2027*.csv. Code: report/build_deck.py, src/hold_scenario.py. Café-level figures: outputs/budget_review_by_cafe.csv.</p>
  {{ foot(SRC) }}
</section>

</body>
